---
import FieldGuideLayout from '../../../components/FieldGuideLayout.astro';
const base = import.meta.env.BASE_URL;
---

<FieldGuideLayout
  title="The opcode menagerie"
  description="88 different opcodes show up across the game's dialog. A handful run the show; the rest are rare. Here's a tour."
  crumbs={[
    { label: 'Field Guide', href: `${base}/field-guide/` },
    { label: 'Message System', href: `${base}/field-guide/message-system/` },
    { label: 'Opcodes' },
  ]}
>
  <p>
    We parsed every dialog entry in the game and counted which
    opcodes appear, how often, and with what arguments. The result:
    <strong>88 distinct opcodes</strong> in actual use. The top ten
    cover almost every entry — the long tail of 78 others appear
    only a handful of times each.
  </p>

  <h2>The frequent ones</h2>
  <p>
    These are the opcodes that drive ordinary dialog. If you read a
    typical NPC line and notice it adjusts to the speaker — using their
    name, their personality particle, their portrait — you're seeing
    these at work.
  </p>

  <table>
    <thead>
      <tr><th>Opcode</th><th>Count</th><th>What it does</th></tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
      <tr>
        <td><code>0x01ff</code></td>
        <td>60,685</td>
        <td>Sentence-ender particle. Each NPC has 6 of them (one per
          personality mode), and this opcode picks one based on the
          speaker. This is why the same Japanese line reads differently
          when said by a peppy character vs a stoic one.</td>
      </tr>
      <tr>
        <td><code>0x0018</code></td>
        <td>59,407</td>
        <td>Speaker portrait ID. Picks which character face appears
          next to the dialog. 129 unique portraits across the game.</td>
      </tr>
      <tr>
        <td><code>0x0017</code></td>
        <td>59,020</td>
        <td>Textbox layout config. 154 distinct configurations of
          textbox size and position.</td>
      </tr>
      <tr>
        <td><code>0x0016</code></td>
        <td>47,314</td>
        <td>Facial expression. 432 different expression IDs.</td>
      </tr>
      <tr>
        <td><code>0x0000</code></td>
        <td>24,540</td>
        <td>Text color. Most common arg is 1 (red, used for emphasis);
          others are blue, green, yellow, pink, orange, gray.</td>
      </tr>
      <tr>
        <td><code>0x0202</code></td>
        <td>21,815</td>
        <td>Per-NPC substitution. Arg 0 inserts the speaker's
          first-person pronoun (わたし/オレ/アタシ); arg 1 inserts their
          personal catchphrase.</td>
      </tr>
      <tr>
        <td><code>0x01f4</code></td>
        <td>10,778</td>
        <td>Player name. Substituted at runtime with whatever name the
          player picked when they started the game.</td>
      </tr>
      <tr>
        <td><code>0x004f</code></td>
        <td>8,070</td>
        <td>Script marker. No arguments. Used to break up dialog into
          logical phrases for the engine.</td>
      </tr>
      <tr>
        <td><code>0x0013</code></td>
        <td>5,870</td>
        <td>Menu choice terminator. Marks the end of one option in a
          multiple-choice prompt.</td>
      </tr>
      <tr>
        <td><code>0x0037</code></td>
        <td>2,526</td>
        <td>Dialog header opcode. Every entry that has a header starts
          with this.</td>
      </tr>
    </tbody>
  </table>

  <h2>The pattern of substitutions</h2>
  <p>
    Many of the most-used opcodes are <strong>text substitutions</strong>:
    placeholders for content that's filled in at runtime. The player's
    name, the speaker's pronoun, the speaker's catchphrase, the
    sentence-ender particle — all are substituted dynamically based on
    who's speaking.
  </p>

  <p>
    For translation, this means we leave the opcode bytes in place
    when we rewrite an entry. The bytes are preserved exactly; the
    text around them gets replaced with English. The engine still
    substitutes the right per-NPC value at runtime. So a translated
    line that reads <code>[PRONOUN] heard about it earlier!</code> in
    our database becomes something like "I heard about it earlier!"
    when spoken by an NPC whose pronoun is "I," or "We heard about it
    earlier!" for a different NPC.
  </p>

  <h2>The 78 less common opcodes</h2>
  <p>
    The other 78 opcodes are a mix of:
  </p>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Branching</strong> — flags that select between dialog
      paths based on game state.</li>
    <li><strong>Inline references</strong> — things like "the name of
      the item the player is currently holding."</li>
    <li><strong>Effects</strong> — sound effects, screen shakes,
      timing pauses.</li>
    <li><strong>Script calls</strong> — invoke an event-script
      sequence from within dialog.</li>
  </ul>

  <p>
    Of the 88 total, about 30 are well understood, 40 are partially
    understood, and the rest are tagged "unknown — appears only a
    few times" and would need targeted investigation to nail down.
    The good news is they're rare, so they don't get in the way of
    most translation work.
  </p>
</FieldGuideLayout>
